AB  - The pervasive challenge of childhood aggression demands innovative, scalable intervention strategies. Traditional parent training programs are effective but often face limitations related to engagement and the ecological validity of practice. This paper presents a VR-AI scoring model to create an immersive, adaptive training environment for parents of aggressive children. This model will be used to score parent feedback to aggressive children in a VR environment. VR’s capacity to simulate high-stress, real-life scenarios provides a safe and engaging platform for skills practice. Critically, the AI system utilizes a Zero-Shot Classification (ZSC) approach, specifically employing the Facebook BART Large MNLI model, to dynamically classify a parent’s free-text response against a set of predefined, clinically derived behavioral labels. This classification score, integrated into a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework, is used to deliver real-time, clinically grounded feedback and drive the virtual child’s adaptive behavior. The paper presents the scientific background of the VR-AI model, where the VR implementation is a second phase in the future work. discusses technological challenges, outlines critical ethical concerns, and presents comparative performance metrics validating the proposed ZSC architecture.
ER  - 

