AB  - In recent years, Virtual Reality (VR) provides a safe and controlled environment for shooting and training, also most existing VR shooting systems offers only basic hit-miss scoring and lack the capability to analyze the shooter’s pre-shot weapon behavior. To overcome these limitations, this research proposed an VR-based Shooting Trainer using Deep Learning and Reinforcement Learning (VST-DLRL). The system captures weapon-mounted IMU data during each shooting attempt and pairs it with VR-recorded shot outcomes, including hit/miss information and deviation from the target center. After preprocessing consisting of noise filtering, normalization, and extraction of a fixed 0.5 -second pre-shot motion window the synchronized data is processed using a hybrid One-Dimensional Convolutional Neural Network - Long Short-Term Memory Network (1D-CNN-LSTM) model. This model learns finegrained weapon movements such as sway, tremor, and trigger jerk, enabling accurate prediction of shot deviation and detection of shooter-induced errors. Real-time predictions drive an VR feedback system that guides the trainee toward increases shooting posture and stability. Also, an adaptive RL module adjusts target size, distance, and motion based on shooting performance and provides personalized progression for both novice and skilled shooters. By integrating VR immersion, motion-driven analysis, and adaptive difficulty control, the proposed system provides a more effective and intelligent framework for improving shooting accuracy. The proposed VST-DLRL framework attained better results in terms of as accuracy (0.92), SSIM (0.96), PSNR (38.46) and pure-capacity (7.8) were compared with existing 6-Degree-of-Freedom (6DOF) Ballistic Simulation Model.
ER  - 

