AB  - Virtual and Augmented Reality (VR/AR) technologies are reshaping healthcare education by enabling immersive, interactive, and risk-free learning environments. This paper presents SurgiX, an adaptive extended reality (XR) framework designed for pre-clinical dental training. The system integrates real-time multimedia rendering, FSM-based procedural control, and a generative scoring engine to provide structured, feedback-driven practice sessions. Developed in Unity 3D with C# scripting, SurgiX operates seamlessly on affordable standalone headsets such as Proculus One and Oculus Quest, offering gesture-based interactions without dependence on proprietary haptics. Through dynamic feedback visualization and adaptive task scaling, the framework reinforces procedural precision and skill retention. A pilot study with 60 dental students demonstrated notable gains in accuracy, efficiency, and error reduction, with strong usability ratings confirming system feasibility. The findings support SurgiX as an accessible and scalable XR solution bridging the gap between theoretical instruction and clinical readiness.
