AB  - With the accelerating trend of population aging, elderly individuals increasingly face age-related sensory degradation, particularly visual and auditory impairments, which significantly hinder their daily activities and medication safety. While traditional smartphone-based assistive applications offer basic age-friendly features, they often impose a "digital fence" characterized by high cognitive friction in interaction paradigms and a severe disconnect from real-time physical environments. To address these critical social pain points, we propose RaySound Vision, a multimodal smart sensory enhancement system deployed on RayNeo X2 augmented reality (AR) glasses. By transforming multi-step manual operations into intuitive, first-person natural interactions, we construct a wearable "sensory agent" that provides barrier-free assistance. Our main technical contributions are three-fold: 1) we design an end-to-cloud streaming architecture to handle intensive multimodal tasks; 2) we implement a customized YOLO and regional OCR pipeline optimized for robust medication identification in complex environments; and 3) we present a comprehensive empirical analysis of system latency across different processing stages (network, local perception, and LLM reasoning). Our real-world evaluations demonstrate that for complex medical reasoning, the system strategically prioritizes semantic fidelity and cognitive safety over raw processing speed, establishing a necessary latency-accuracy trade-off that effectively reconstructs a reliable and accessible sensory world for the elderly.
ER  - 

