AB  - As engineering education continues to grow, integrating emerging technologies into the learning process is paramount to meet the demands of modern industries. This paper developed an Educational Cyber-Physical System (ECPS) designed to link the gap between theoretical knowledge and practical application in future engineering education. The proposed system supports a wide range of engineering disciplines, including mechanical, electrical, civil, and computer engineering, allowing for multidisciplinary applications. By combining artificial intelligence (AI), augmented reality (AR), and the internet of things (IoT), students can investigate different engineering systems in a dynamic yet regulated setting. The ECPS framework enables real-time feedback, personalized learning paths, and collaborative problem-solving, fostering a deeper connection between theory and practice. The proposed work is that an AI-powered computer assistant will automatically oversee every step of the student education process, enabling prompt modifications in response to the student's development. The assistant's capacity to gather thorough data on every student activity within the programme is essential to this procedure, especially when it comes to monitoring pedagogical actions in real-time and making any necessary adjustments. This makes efficient use of ECPS to accumulate all pertinent records and extract pertinent information. This article looks at a particular instance of real-world work where students from two different places work together in a combined learning setting.
ER  - 

